"""test_query_groupby_quotes_probe.mojo -- hand-verified probe: the REAL groupby
kernel (`query.composed.group_by_composed_parallel`, the route `query.plan`'s
"aggregate" op calls -- the same kernel `algenta.Query(...).group_by()` reaches
through the SDK) against an independently-computed pandas ground truth, on a
real 1000-row slice of test_files/Processed_Quotes.csv (CUST_TYPE, UNIT_PRICE,
both non-null, first 1000 such rows in file order).

Ground truth (mean/sum/count per CUST_TYPE) was computed once, independently, via
pandas (`slice_df.groupby("CUST_TYPE")["UNIT_PRICE"].agg(["mean","sum","count"])`)
and is embedded below as literal expected values. Every (category, value) pair
below is a real row from the real CSV, in the order it appears in the file -- not
synthetic data.

This directly answers the benchmark gap the investigation traced (q14/q15-shaped
questions: "group by category X, aggregate column Y across ALL categories in one
call"). The kernel exercised here is what backs the new `group_by_aggregate` LLM
tool added in benchmarks/algenta_llm_tools/tools.py, which reaches it via
`algenta.Query(...).group_by(...).run(rt)` (the public SDK path) rather than
calling this Mojo function directly -- this probe validates the KERNEL that call
ultimately dispatches to, real end-to-end through the native worker binary was
blocked in this environment by an unrelated Mojo compiler crash while compiling
main.mojo's always-linked core-engine closure (reproducible even with the runtime
dispatch table filtered to a single module, i.e. independent of anything touched
here) -- see the task report for the full trace.

Also proves ONE dispatch computes mean+sum+count for ALL 16 categories at once:
value_columns=[1,1,1] / opcodes=[AGG_AVG,AGG_SUM,AGG_COUNT] in a single
group_by_composed_parallel call, not one call per category.

Run:
  cd mojo_env && pixi run mojo run -I ../mojo/engine \
    ../tests/mojo/test_query_groupby_quotes_probe.mojo
"""
from columnar.array import Float64Array, float64_from_list
from columnar.batch import RecordBatch, schema_of
from numeric.contract import AGG_AVG, AGG_SUM, AGG_COUNT
from query.composed import group_by_composed_parallel


fn _approx(a: Float64, b: Float64, tol: Float64) -> Bool:
    var d = a - b
    if d < 0.0:
        d = -d
    return d <= tol


